## Solution

**Setup.** Let the original triangle have vertices $A_1, A_2, A_3$ (Sites 1, 2, 3). Each side $A_iA_{i+1}$ is divided into four equal parts by markers at $\tfrac14, \tfrac12, \tfrac34$ from $A_i$.

- $B_i$ is the marker closest to $A_i$: at $\tfrac14$ from $A_i$, i.e., $B_i = \tfrac34 A_i + \tfrac14 A_{i+1}$.
- $C_i$ is the marker closest to $A_{i+1}$: at $\tfrac34$ from $A_i$, i.e., $C_i = \tfrac14 A_i + \tfrac34 A_{i+1}$.

**Coordinates.** Take $A_1=(0,0),\; A_2=(1,0),\; A_3=(0,1)$, so the original area is $\tfrac12$.

$$\begin{aligned}
B_1&=\bigl(\tfrac14,0\bigr), & B_2&=\bigl(\tfrac34,\tfrac14\bigr), & B_3&=\bigl(0,\tfrac34\bigr),\\
C_1&=\bigl(\tfrac34,0\bigr), & C_2&=\bigl(\tfrac14,\tfrac34\bigr), & C_3&=\bigl(0,\tfrac14\bigr).
\end{aligned}$$

Each inner triangle has area ratio $1-3\cdot\tfrac14\cdot\tfrac34 = \tfrac{7}{16}$ (verified by shoelace on $\triangle B$: area $= \tfrac{7}{32}$, ratio $\tfrac{7}{16}$).

**Finding the overlap.** No vertex of $\triangle B$ lies inside $\triangle C$ (and vice versa), so the overlap is a hexagon. Computing all six edge-intersections:

| Point | Intersection | Coordinates |
|---|---|---|
| $P_1$ | $B_1B_2 \cap C_1C_2$ | $(\tfrac58,\tfrac{3}{16})$ |
| $P_2$ | $B_1B_2 \cap C_3C_1$ | $(\tfrac{9}{20},\tfrac{1}{10})$ |
| $P_3$ | $B_2B_3 \cap C_1C_2$ | $(\tfrac{9}{20},\tfrac{9}{20})$ |
| $P_4$ | $B_2B_3 \cap C_2C_3$ | $(\tfrac{3}{16},\tfrac{5}{8})$ |
| $P_5$ | $B_3B_1 \cap C_2C_3$ | $(\tfrac{1}{10},\tfrac{9}{20})$ |
| $P_6$ | $B_3B_1 \cap C_3C_1$ | $(\tfrac{3}{16},\tfrac{3}{16})$ |

Tracing the boundary (alternating between edges of $B$ and $C$), the hexagon in order is:
$$P_2 \to P_1 \to P_3 \to P_4 \to P_5 \to P_6 \to P_2.$$

**Shoelace area.** Converting to denominator $80$:

$$P_2=\bigl(\tfrac{36}{80},\tfrac{8}{80}\bigr),\; P_1=\bigl(\tfrac{50}{80},\tfrac{15}{80}\bigr),\; P_3=\bigl(\tfrac{36}{80},\tfrac{36}{80}\bigr),\; P_4=\bigl(\tfrac{15}{80},\tfrac{50}{80}\bigr),\; P_5=\bigl(\tfrac{8}{80},\tfrac{36}{80}\bigr),\; P_6=\bigl(\tfrac{15}{80},\tfrac{15}{80}\bigr).$$

$$\sum(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i) = 140+1260+1260+140-420-420 = 1960 \quad\text{(in units of }1/80^2\text{)}.$$

$$\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1960}{6400} = \frac{980}{6400} = \frac{49}{320}.$$

**Ratio to original triangle:**

$$\frac{49/320}{1/2} = \frac{49}{160}.$$

**Consistency check.** $|\triangle B|+|\triangle C| = 2\cdot\tfrac{7}{16} = \tfrac{140}{160}$; overlap $=\tfrac{49}{160}$; union $= \tfrac{91}{160} < 1$. ✓

$$\boxed{\dfrac{49}{160}}$$

